        "header": "Chapter 10. Lists",
        "blocks": [
            {
                "type": "block",
                "code": ">>> t = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f']\n>>> t[1:3]\n['b', 'c']\n>>> t[:4]\n['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']\n>>> t[3:]\n['d', 'e', 'f']",
                "language": "python",
                "description": null
            },
            {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "If you omit the first index, the slice starts at the beginning. If you omit the second, the slice goes to the end. So if you omit both, the slice is a copy of the whole list."
            },
            {
                "type": "block",
                "code": ">>> t[:]\n['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f']",
                "language": "python",
                "description": null
            },
            {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Since lists are mutable, it is often useful to make a copy before performing operations that modify lists.\n\nA slice operator on the left side of an assignment can update multiple elements:"
            },
            {
                "type": "block",
                "code": ">>> t = ['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e', 'f']\n>>> t[1:3] = ['x', 'y']\n>>> t\n['a', 'x', 'y', 'd', 'e', 'f']",
                "language": "python",
                "description": null
            },
            {
                "type": "section_heading",
                "title": "10.6 List methods"
            },
            {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Python provides methods that operate on lists. For example, append adds a new element to the end of a list:"
            },
            {
                "type": "block",
                "code": ">>> t = ['a', 'b', 'c']\n>>> t.append('d')\n>>> t\n['a', 'b', 'c', 'd']",
                "language": "python",
                "description": null
            },
            {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "extend takes a list as an argument and appends all of the elements:"
            },
            {
                "type": "block",
                "code": ">>> t1 = ['a', 'b', 'c']\n>>> t2 = ['d', 'e']\n>>> t1.extend(t2)\n>>> t1\n['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']",
                "language": "python",
                "description": null
            },
            {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "This example leaves t2 unmodified.\n\nsort arranges the elements of the list from low to high:"
            },
            {
                "type": "block",
                "code": ">>> t = ['d', 'c', 'e', 'b', 'a']\n>>> t.sort()\n>>> t\n['a', 'b', 'c', 'd', 'e']",
                "language": "python",
                "description": null
            },
            {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Most list methods are void; they modify the list and return None. If you accidentally write t = t.sort(), you will be disappointed with the result."
            }
        ]
    },
    "cost": 0.042765
